From 20 to 21 October 1999, the [[People's Consultative Assembly]] (MPR), the [[legislature|legislative branch]] of [[Indonesia]], met to elect both the [[President of Indonesia|president]] and [[Vice President of Indonesia|vice president]] of the country for the 1999–2004 term. The incumbent president, [[B. J. Habibie]], had ascended after the resignation of longtime president [[Suharto]] in 1998, but declined to stand for a full term after his accountability speech was voted down. On 20 October, [[Abdurrahman Wahid]], chairman of the [[Nahdlatul Ulama]], was elected president and [[Inauguration of Abdurrahman Wahid|inaugurated]] on the same day. Wahid's opponent, [[Megawati Sukarnoputri]] was subsequently elected vice president the next day.<ref>{{cite news |title=Pemilihan Presiden dari Masa ke Masa |url=https://news.detik.com/berita/d-2723006/pemilihan-presiden-dari-masa-ke-masa |access-date=26 October 2021 |agency=Detik News |publisher=news.Detik.com |date=Oct 19, 2014 |archive-date=26 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211026100446/https://news.detik.com/berita/d-2723006/pemilihan-presiden-dari-masa-ke-masa |url-status=dead }}</ref> The elections represented the first relatively democratic and [[peaceful transfer of power]] in the [[history of Indonesia]].<ref>{{cite book | author = National Democratic Institute | author-link = National Democratic Institute | date = 28 November 1999 | title = The 1999 Presidential Election and Post-Election Developments in Indonesia: a Post Election Assessment Report | url = https://www.ndi.org/node/21592 | publisher = [[National Democratic Institute]] | page = 4 | archive-date = 7 December 2024 | access-date = 15 December 2022 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20241207095812/https://www.ndi.org/node/21592 | url-status = dead }}</ref> It was also the first presidential election decided by counting of votes.

==Background==
In October, the [[People's Consultative Assembly]] made up of the [[People's Representative Council]] and 200 nominated members from the military and selected civilians, a total of 700, met to elect the [[President of Indonesia|president]] and [[Vice President of Indonesia|vice president]].

There were initially four candidates for the presidency; [[Abdurrahman Wahid]], [[B. J. Habibie]], [[Megawati Sukarnoputri]], and [[Yusril Ihza Mahendra]]. However, Habibie refused the nomination from [[Golkar]] after his accountability speech was rejected by the [[People's Consultative Assembly|MPR]] the day before election, while Yusril withdrew his candidacy on election day.<ref>{{cite book |author1=Djadja Suparman |title=Jejak kudeta, 1997-2005 catatan harian Letnan Jenderal (Purn) TNI Djadja Suparman |date=2013 |publisher=Yayasan Pustaka Obor Indonesia |isbn=9789794618370 |page=208 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lxQaDAAAQBAJ&dq=Yusril+Ihza+mundur+dari+kandidat+presiden+1999&pg=PA208 |access-date=26 October 2021}}</ref> 

==Election day==
On 20 October, PKB chairman [[Abdurrahman Wahid]] was elected, beating [[Megawati Sukarnoputri]] by 373 votes to 313, although her party ([[Indonesian Democratic Party of Struggle|PDI-P]]) won the most votes in the legislative election and had one-third of the parliamentary seats. This triggered riots among Megawati's supporters. The following day, Megawati was nominated by Gus Dur's party ([[National Awakening Party|PKB]]) as vice president and got elected, beating [[Hamzah Haz]] from the [[United Development Party|PPP]] by 396 votes to 284. This ended the street protests.<ref name="Friend4612">Friend, Theodore (2003) ''Indonesian Destinies'', The Belknap Press of Harvard University Press, pp. 461–462 {{ISBN|978-0-6740113-7-3}}</ref><ref name="RICKLEFS539">{{cite book  | last = Ricklefs | first = M.C  | title = A History of Modern Indonesia Since c. 1200 | publisher = Palgrave Macmillan  | edition = Fourth  | date = 2008  | pages = 539  | isbn = 978-0-230-54685-1}}</ref>

This was the first and only democratically indirect presidential election in Indonesia and the first presidential election that did not feature a candidate from the ruling party ([[Golkar]]).

